1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,960
 delays. It puts more traffic on the side streets where children and seniors are. It puts more

2
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:16,320
 traffic on Monkland and Summerled. There's a lot of problems with this bike path and EXP did a

3
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:22,500
 flawed study in which they only studied the area for two days and didn't ask the right questions.

4
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:32,120
 So if we can keep the bike path as is and do a proper study where NDG residents have their

5
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:39,880
 say, that would be greatly appreciated. Would this council be willing to hold such a public

6
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:46,520
 consultation to understand the true nature and views of the residents, whatever the solution

7
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:50,460
 is and whatever the majority says?

8
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:55,560
 Thank you again, Erwin, for your proposition. But I think our administration has been very

9
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:57,440
 clear with where we want to go on the bike path.

10
00:00:57,840 --> 00:00:58,400
 Thank you.

11
00:00:58,660 --> 00:00:59,020
 Thank you.

12
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:04,380
 Stephen Jass?

13
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:05,180
 Mr. Jass?

14
00:01:17,180 --> 00:01:29,460
 I'm not going to make many new friends here tonight. I appreciate that the previous administration,

15
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:36,440
 Projet Montréal, wanted to put bike paths here and there all across the city. I have no issues

16
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:45,080
 with that. But in this instance, without any consultation whatsoever, bam! It was pre-decided

17
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:55,140
 we were going to uproot all of Terrebonne, and not just put in a bike path, put in two bike paths. Go figure.

18
00:01:56,140 --> 00:02:04,300
 So sure, this benefits the dozens of cyclists currently. No, I'll be generous. The hundreds of cyclists

19
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:15,360
 that use Terrebonne now. But what about the thousand people who live on Terrebonne and everybody in the surrounding area?

20
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:28,360
 I would say that's easily 10,000 people. So we're benefiting a few hundred, but we're just totally uprooting and changing everything for the tens of thousands of people here in NDG.

21
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:38,420
 Never mind that there's no parking in front of Willingdon, or St. Monica's School, or St. Monica's Church, or even the Mackay Center.

22
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:46,480
 So here are three examples, because this is for Mr. Alex, because maybe he's unaware of this. I did this a couple years ago.

23
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:58,540
 So as an example, if we are here at the Provigo on Monkland, and we just want to go to the next street over, that's what we used to be able to do.

24
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:08,600
 No can do anymore. You can't even go down to Summerlet to turn left. You have to be going all the way to Girouard and do this whole rigmarole.

25
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:17,600
 So whereas, you could just do this. Now, nine intersections, three stop signs, three stoplights, eight speed bumps.

26
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:28,660
 To go from one side of Cavendish to the other. Very simply, this is St. Monica's here, but just imagine Besbro, to go from here to there.

27
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:37,660
 That's what happened in the past. Now, people have to go all the way to Kensington, all the way up, and deal with the traffic mayhem of Monkland.

28
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:42,760
 Never mind the disaster of Monkland and Cavendish, and come back down to go there.

29
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:50,720
 Here was one intersection. Now there are nine intersections, four stop signs, two traffic lights, three speed bumps.

30
00:03:50,720 --> 00:04:02,720
 And one time when I made this presentation, there was a lady who lived here on Melrose to drive her kid to Willingdon, and then go on to Loyola.

31
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:13,780
 No can do. Now she'd have to go all the way to Summerlet, all the way to Hampton, to come all the way back up Terrebonne, to go to the front of Willingdon, and do the whole loop all over again.

32
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:20,780
 Fourteen intersections, eight stop signs, one traffic light, nine speed bumps.

33
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:29,780
 So in all of this, I'm hoping to have a good answer. I understand how this benefits the hundreds of cyclists.

34
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:36,840
 How does this benefit the tens of thousands of other residents around Terrebonne?

35
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:44,840
 Because this little tweak of a bicycle path, it's still going to be two bike paths, basically, throughout the year.

36
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:47,880
 All we're doing is changing something for three months, possibly.

37
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:57,780
 But please, rather than address the hundreds of cyclists, can you address the tens of thousands of residents around NDG who are affected by this, please?

38
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:04,840
 Hi, Stephen. It's always a pleasure to see your presentation. Very well done. I really enjoy it.

39
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:11,900
 I really enjoy it. It's a very complicated question, finding this middle ground, as our mayor was saying, all through the night.

40
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:17,900
 We need to consider the needs of all the residents, all ages, all abilities.

41
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:21,900
 To be perfectly frank, personally, I think most of you know that I'm a cyclist.

42
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:28,960
 I move around the city 80% of the time on the back of my bike, and that's even through winter.

43
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:31,960
 I teach my kids how to ride on Terrebonne.

44
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:37,960
 So as many, many parents here, I think it's important to have a safe infrastructure.

45
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:43,020
 And like our mayor said, we can do more and better. This is our long-term plan.

46
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:50,080
 But at the same time, we also need to take in consideration the needs and the request that we've had for years

47
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:58,140
 of those thousands of residents who also need their needs met in terms of accessibility and comfort of life.

48
00:05:58,140 --> 00:06:04,200
 And the social acceptability, unfortunately, was not taken into consideration by the previous administration.

49
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:07,200
 And we promised to listen and to act. That's what we're doing.

50
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:13,200
 I hear you on the fact that some drivers might be inconvenienced.

51
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:18,200
 And sometimes when I do have to take the car, I know exactly what you're talking about,

52
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:21,200
 that you need to do a round like this when you can go straight.

53
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:29,260
 I think that's, you know, give or take. And the vivre ensemble imposes that, that we all have to put de l'eau dans notre vin.

54
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:34,260
 And that's what we're going to do and find that very difficult middle ground that is tough to navigate.

55
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:40,260
 But it's thanks to conversations like these that will be able to move past the opposition.

56
00:06:40,260 --> 00:06:43,260
 And I'll finish by saying something that is really amazing.

57
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:49,260
 I was biking way before I decided to, you know, get into local politics.

58
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:55,320
 And I never realized how much for a lot of, you know, for a certain group of people, it's a political act.

59
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:59,320
 You know, the way you move around the city and it doesn't have to be this way.

60
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:05,520
 I really hope that we're able to move past those differences that some people are trying to exacerbate,

61
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:11,600
 especially online by saying things that are very questionable, attacking people's integrity.

62
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:31,400
 And I'm not going to debate more on that, but I really hope that we're able to show, you know, Montrealers and indigenous residents that we're able to have a reasonable compromise approach that will help all residents using all kinds of mobility to improve the way they move around the city in an inclusive way.

63
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:33,600
 There you go.

64
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,420
 Thank you for the answer.

65
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:39,720
 I just want to say, middle ground, quote unquote.

66
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:41,720
 This is not middle ground.

67
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,720
 This is 90%.

68
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,720
 Everything remains the same.

69
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:49,720
 Unidirectional, all the parking things.

70
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:52,720
 There's only a little tweak for three months.

71
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:58,580
 What I want to ask, I want to understand this properly for the benefit of even the cyclists here.

72
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:03,100
 You're potentially going to a vote later on today.

73
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:07,240
 There's going to go to a vote down at City Hall.

74
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:18,680
 But that's only really to study the measure because after this, doesn't there need to be a real study as to how this is going to happen,

75
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:22,080
 determine what needs to be done?

76
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:25,220
 It needs maybe not necessarily an engineering study.

77
00:08:25,340 --> 00:08:27,280
 Maybe that's what the EXP is about.

78
00:08:28,020 --> 00:08:31,000
 But the city services have to sign off on it.

79
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:33,060
 Engineers have to sign off on it.

80
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:37,740
 And then doesn't this all come back for a whole new re-vote?

81
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:45,940
 Because right now we're just voting in abstract, and it's not really determined what's going to happen.

82
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:53,660
 So, can you clarify us all on the procedures or what's coming up, please?

83
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:55,320
 Thank you so much, Stephen.

84
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:59,760
 So, basically, it's called en 85, c'est une délégation de pouvoir.

85
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:04,000
 It's a delegation of power that technically should belong to the agglomeration.

86
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:14,080
 In the previous mandate, it was the same process that was put in place where us, as a council, we have to ask City Hall and the agglomeration,

87
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:18,480
 is it okay if we make this change on our territory?

88
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:27,080
 So, again, there is no infrastructure or aménagement that will be put in place that is not conformed to the rules,

89
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,340
 and that is not signed by an engineer.

90
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:34,920
 So, whatever we're putting in place has to be signed by an engineer, has to be approved by a professional,

91
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:41,680
 and has to fit les normes conformes des règlements in place.

92
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:45,720
 Sorry, I'm doing a bit of franglet just in this intervention.

93
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,880
 So, how it works, again, with the délégation de pouvoir, we're asking, again, City Hall.

94
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,720
 So, that's why there's three votes specifically to this kind of request that we're making.

95
00:09:56,260 --> 00:10:06,020
 Tonight, what we're voting on is just the approval that we're going to be able to vote on this in December.

96
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:07,440
 So, we're sending it over.

97
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:09,340
 The mechanism is we're sending it over.

98
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:20,000
 We're voting today as a council to vote in favor or not of sending it to City Hall for the council meeting of November or December 1st.

